* Weak two-bids

Booklet section "SEQUENCE AFTER A WEAK TWO-BID OF 2D, 2H OR 2S". A weak two shows a
six-card suit of reasonable quality and 5--11 HCP. The booklet also allows a good
five-card suit or a poor seven-card suit "on rare occasions"; this file keeps the
length at exactly six and treats those judgment calls as out of scope (NOTES.md
#3). 2NT is forcing, asking for a minimum/maximum (and, opposite a maximum, a
feature -- see NOTES.md #4); any raise is to play; a new suit shows 10+ hcp and
a five-card suit, forcing one round ("RONF"), and -- like any other new suit --
is a suit ranked *above* the opening at the cheapest available level (2-level)
or, for a suit ranked below it, one level higher (review finding: this file
previously wrote every new suit at the 3 level regardless of rank, so the two
suits actually ranked above each of 2D and 2H were an unwarranted, undocumented
jump instead of the plain 2-level bid RONF describes; see `NOTES.md` #24).

Every response has a real range; nothing is a catch-all, so a hand that fits
none of them passes (the implicit pass, NOTES.md "Notrump lane" #N5). A raise
to game with four-card support is the further preempt (5--14 hcp); a
three-card raise to the 3 level is competitive (6--14 hcp); 2NT asks with 15+
hcp and at least a doubleton; 3NT is to play with 15+ hcp and shortness in
opener's suit. After a new suit, opener raises with three-card support, bids
2NT (or 3NT at the 3 level) with a maximum, and otherwise rebids his suit.

2D = {prio:63} 6=!d, 5--11 hcp
2H = {prio:64} 6=!h, 5--11 hcp
2S = {prio:65} 6=!s, 5--11 hcp

